# A generative bloom of pulsating rings, driven by the tick counter alone —
# no input, no state threaded across ticks (A.6). Motion needs no event: the
# whole picture is a pure function of `tick`, recomputed fresh on every call.
#
# `mod` comes from math.planes (checkpoint v21.0 §250 — harvested from this
# file and snake.planes, two witnesses of three, floored rather than either
# witness's original truncated definition). The local ramp-up-ramp-down wave
# `mod`'s snap-back-to-zero gets folded into stayed local — one witness only,
# and the harvest rule does not admit on one — but it is named `pulse` here
# rather than its old name `triangle`: that name now belongs to draw.planes's
# shape verb, and a module graph cannot hold two functions of the same name.
#
# Colour is OKLCH throughout (planes-drawing-protocol-v1.md §4): the whole
# palette's hue rotates with `tick`, and each ring is offset from the last by
# a fixed 18 degrees — an analogous harmony spreading outward through the
# rings, computed by addition on hue rather than hand-mixed per-channel
# arithmetic, and wrapped with the same floored `mod` hue arithmetic needs.
#
# What alpha changes here is what the program *is*. Opaque strokes occlude:
# ten of them is ten outlines stacked front-to-back, and the eye reads the
# stacking order. A low-alpha fill under a higher-alpha edge mixes instead,
# and ten of those read as one glazed thing made of light. Independent fill
# and stroke, and any alpha between 0 and 1, are what make that expressible;
# neither existed in this corpus before.
#
# The petal is the only path in the corpus. Everything else the protocol can
# draw is a shape it already names — a circle, a rectangle, an ellipse. A
# petal is not, and never will be: `shape`/`vertex`/`curve`/`close`/`end` is
# how the vocabulary stops being a fixed menu. It is drawn once at a fixed
# size, in its own frame with its base at the origin, and `push`/`rotate`/
# `scale`/`pop` puts it where it belongs — so `petal` takes no arguments at
# all and the transform stack carries every difference between one petal and
# the next.

use draw
use math

let ring-count = 10
let ring-period = 48 because "the pulse's half-period in ticks: at sixty frames a second a ring swells and falls back roughly every 1.6 seconds, slow enough to read as breathing rather than flicker"
let ring-stagger = 12 because "each ring's pulse is offset twelve ticks from the last, so the swell travels outward through the rings instead of all ten beating in lockstep"
let ring-harmony = 18 because "an analogous harmony: each ring eighteen degrees further round the hue circle than the last (planes-drawing-protocol-v1.md §4.1 — 25 degrees is the classic analogous step, and a little under it keeps ten rings inside one family)"
let petal-turn = 36 because "ten petals thirty-six degrees apart close the circle exactly once, so no petal doubles another"

to pulse of t, half-period:
  let folded = mod of t, (half-period * 2)
  if folded < half-period:
    give folded
  else:
    give (half-period * 2) - folded

# The petal: base at the origin, tip straight up the negative y axis, drawn
# once at one fixed size. Two cubics out and back, closed at the base.
to petal:
  shape
  vertex of 0, 0
  curve of -19, -14, -13, -39, 0, -46
  curve of 13, -39, 19, -14, 0, 0
  close
  end

to rings of ring-index, tick, base-hue:
  if ring-index >= ring-count:
    give nothing
  else:
    let offset = ring-index * ring-stagger
    let phase = pulse of (tick + offset), ring-period
    let glow = phase / ring-period because "0 at the pulse's trough and 1 at its crest — one fraction, and radius, lightness and both alphas are all read off it"
    let radius = 20 + phase * 2.6 because "twenty pixels at the trough and a hundred and forty-five at the crest: the widest ring nearly fills the 360-tall canvas, so the bloom is the picture rather than a motif floating in a lot of empty ground"
    let lightness = 0.45 + glow * 0.35 because "the pulse modulates lightness, in place of the old hand-mixed red and green channels"
    let hue = mod of (base-hue + ring-index * ring-harmony), 360 because "each ring further round the wheel than the last, wrapped by the shared floored mod so a hue is always a point on the circle"

    let body-alpha = 0.06 + glow * 0.09 because "an ABSOLUTE swing of nine hundredths, not a proportion of the 0.06 base: a ring at 0.06 alpha animated by a *fraction* of 0.06 moves by thousandths, which is invisible, and the canvas looks frozen. Translucency has to be animated in alpha units"
    let edge-alpha = 0.35 + glow * 0.3 because "the edge carries four to six times the body's alpha, so a ring reads as a bounded thing full of light rather than as a soft blur with no shape"

    fill of lightness, 0.13, hue, body-alpha
    stroke of (lightness + 0.15), 0.19, hue, edge-alpha
    circle of 0, 0, radius

    let petal-scale = radius / 64 because "the petal is drawn once at one size and scaled into place, so it breathes with its ring — and because scale carries the pen with it, the outline thins as the petal shrinks instead of staying a fixed weight"
    let petal-angle = mod of (ring-index * petal-turn + tick * 2), 360 because "the whole flower turns two degrees a tick, wrapped so the emitted angle stays a number a reader can hold rather than climbing forever"

    push
    rotate of petal-angle
    scale of petal-scale, petal-scale
    petal
    pop

    rings of (ring-index + 1), tick, base-hue

clear

let ground-lightness = 0.17 because "luminous colour needs a dark substrate: translucent fills over white wash out toward white however they are mixed, and the whole point of the low alphas above is that they add up to light"
let ground-chroma = 0.03
let ground-hue = 265
background of ground-lightness, ground-chroma, ground-hue

# The petal is one closed path with a real joint where its two curves meet at
# the tip; a round join is what keeps that tip from growing a spike as the
# outline thickens.
corner of "round"
width of 2

let drift-x = pulse of tick, 110 because "a smooth ramp from 0 to 110 and back, so the bloom's centre drifts sideways instead of sitting still"
let drift-y = pulse of (tick + 30), 70 because "offsetting the vertical drift's phase from the horizontal one is what keeps the centre off a single straight line"
let center-x = 215 + drift-x * 0.5 because "halving the drift keeps the widest ring inside the 480-wide canvas at both ends of the ramp"
let center-y = 155 + drift-y * 0.5

let base-hue = mod of (tick * 2), 360 because "the whole palette rotates through hue over time, wrapping at 360 the same way the per-ring offset does"

# The whole bloom is drawn at the origin and placed once, the way turtle's
# tree is: every ring, every petal and the highlight arc below are written in
# the flower's own coordinates, and where it sits on the page is one
# translate. Nothing inside knows the canvas exists.
push
translate of center-x, center-y

rings of 0, tick, base-hue

# One arc, outside the rings: a highlight sweeping round the bloom's widest
# edge. An arc is a curve and not a wedge (planes-drawing-protocol-v1.md §7),
# so this is a bright stroke of exactly 120 degrees and nothing is filled.
let outer-phase = pulse of (tick + (ring-count - 1) * ring-stagger), ring-period
let outer-radius = 20 + outer-phase * 2.6
let sweep-start = mod of (tick * 4), 360
let sweep-end = mod of (sweep-start + 120), 360 because "wrapping the end means it lands at or below the start for a third of every revolution, which is the case §7 pins: add 360 to the end until it exceeds the start, so the sweep is 120 degrees clockwise either way"
stroke of 0.97, 0.11, base-hue, 0.75
width of 3
arc of 0, 0, (outer-radius + 9), sweep-start, sweep-end

pop
